And then I want to apply that
simple consequence to get

248
00:20:11,810 --> 00:20:15,690
a lot of other consequences.

249
00:20:15,690 --> 00:20:20,610
So the first thing
I want to do is

250
00:20:20,610 --> 00:20:24,460
what's called Rolle's
lemma or theorem.

251
00:20:41,360 --> 00:20:46,760
So this is the following,
that I have a function

252
00:20:46,760 --> 00:20:52,390
f from a to b into.

253
00:20:55,360 --> 00:20:57,760
R.

254
00:20:57,760 --> 00:21:00,860
And I'm assuming that
f is differentiable.

255
00:21:08,920 --> 00:21:14,080
And I'm also assuming
that the value initially

256
00:21:14,080 --> 00:21:19,600
and the value at the end, that
those two values are the same.

257
00:21:19,600 --> 00:21:22,340
Sometimes one set
those value equal to 0,

258
00:21:22,340 --> 00:21:26,800
but they just need
to be the same.

259
00:21:26,800 --> 00:21:31,950
And then the claim is
that then there exist.

260
00:21:36,188 --> 00:21:47,080
a x0, where is strictly
between a and b.

261
00:21:47,080 --> 00:21:54,410
And the derivative at
this x0 is equal to 0.

262
00:21:57,024 --> 00:21:59,960
And so the idea is
just very simple,

263
00:21:59,960 --> 00:22:16,080
that you have a function.

264
00:22:16,080 --> 00:22:18,160
And the function initially--

265
00:22:18,160 --> 00:22:20,240
this is set a.

266
00:22:20,240 --> 00:22:22,000
Here, you have b.

267
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:25,380
The function initially
and at the end

268
00:22:25,380 --> 00:22:27,020
end up at the same value.

269
00:22:27,020 --> 00:22:35,250
So this here is f of a, and
that's the same as f of b.

270
00:22:38,230 --> 00:22:42,670
And then who knows what
happened in between?

271
00:22:42,670 --> 00:22:44,070
Maybe like this.

272
00:22:44,070 --> 00:22:46,830
And the claim is
that then there's

273
00:22:46,830 --> 00:22:49,890
always some point where
the derivative is 0.

274
00:22:49,890 --> 00:22:52,830
In this case, it looks
like that those two

275
00:22:52,830 --> 00:22:56,830
would be the points where
the derivative was 0.

276
00:22:56,830 --> 00:23:02,870
And the proof is just
this proof that there's

277
00:23:02,870 --> 00:23:04,120
three cases to consider.

278
00:23:14,910 --> 00:23:16,870
One is-- I mean,
you don't really

279
00:23:16,870 --> 00:23:19,270
have to divide it up by
this, but let me divide it up

280
00:23:19,270 --> 00:23:20,650
into these three cases.

281
00:23:20,650 --> 00:23:27,260
One is that the function
here is constant, everywhere

282
00:23:27,260 --> 00:23:30,180
equal to the same.

283
00:23:30,180 --> 00:23:32,340
Well, if the
function's constant,

284
00:23:32,340 --> 00:23:34,600
then the derivative is 0.

285
00:23:34,600 --> 00:23:36,500
We have already proven that.

286
00:23:36,500 --> 00:23:52,620
Case 2, somewhere, there exist
x such that the value at this x

287
00:23:52,620 --> 00:23:56,800
is strictly bigger than
the value at the endpoints.

288
00:23:59,500 --> 00:24:03,020
And then case 3--

289
00:24:03,020 --> 00:24:09,340
there exists an x such
that the value at this x

290
00:24:09,340 --> 00:24:12,810
is strictly less than the
values at the endpoint.

291
00:24:15,340 --> 00:24:20,000
It's clear that one of these
three cases happen, right?

292
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:25,140
Of course, it could be that
case 2 and 3 could both happen.

293
00:24:25,140 --> 00:24:29,620
But at least one of the
three cases would happen.

294
00:24:29,620 --> 00:24:34,260
In case 1, we already know that
the derivative is 0 everywhere.

295
00:24:34,260 --> 00:24:36,180
So let's just look at case 2.

296
00:24:36,180 --> 00:24:38,420
Case 3 is similar.

297
00:24:38,420 --> 00:24:42,560
So if there exists some
x where the value--

298
00:24:42,560 --> 00:24:46,380
so in this picture here,
this here is maybe x.

299
00:24:46,380 --> 00:24:51,900
The value here is larger
than at the two endpoints.

300
00:24:51,900 --> 00:24:54,980
Well, by the extreme
value theorem

301
00:24:54,980 --> 00:24:57,500
that we proved earlier--
just because if a function is

302
00:24:57,500 --> 00:24:59,620
differentiable, it's
also continuous.

303
00:24:59,620 --> 00:25:01,780
By the extreme
value theorem, there

304
00:25:01,780 --> 00:25:05,620
exists somewhere where,
actually, the maximum

305
00:25:05,620 --> 00:25:07,166
is achieved.

306
00:25:07,166 --> 00:25:10,940
And since somewhere in between--

307
00:25:10,940 --> 00:25:16,580
in this case here,
the maximum is not

308
00:25:16,580 --> 00:25:19,240
achieved at the two endpoints.

309
00:25:19,240 --> 00:25:26,025
So the maximum must be
achieved at an interior point.

310
00:25:26,025 --> 00:25:28,950
But now, it's not
just a local maximum.

311
00:25:28,950 --> 00:25:30,590
It actually is a maximum.

312
00:25:30,590 --> 00:25:32,770
But already, if it
was a local maximum,

313
00:25:32,770 --> 00:25:35,610
the derivative would
be 0 at that point.

314
00:25:35,610 --> 00:25:37,330
So this proved case 2.

315
00:25:37,330 --> 00:25:41,090
And case 3 is similar.

316
00:25:41,090 --> 00:25:45,930
So this is Rolle's theorem
Now, Rolle's theorem

317
00:25:45,930 --> 00:25:49,730
have then a bunch
of applications.

318
00:25:49,730 --> 00:25:53,900
And the first one is to what's
called the mean, value theorem.

319
00:25:57,570 --> 00:26:01,090
And there are two versions
of the mean value theorem.

320
00:26:01,090 --> 00:26:04,810
One is the actual
mean value theorem.

321
00:26:04,810 --> 00:26:08,530
And the other is like
a fancy version of it,

322
00:26:08,530 --> 00:26:11,890
where you have two functions
in play at the same time.

323
00:26:11,890 --> 00:26:15,810
And that's called the
Cauchy mean value theorem.

324
00:26:15,810 --> 00:26:28,030
So first consequence
of Rolle's theorem.

325
00:26:33,530 --> 00:26:35,880
And this is the
mean value theorem.

326
00:26:41,750 --> 00:26:42,250
OK.

327
00:26:42,250 --> 00:26:45,760
And so the first one is the
actual mean value theorem.

328
00:26:55,530 --> 00:26:57,370
And the actual
mean value theorem

329
00:26:57,370 --> 00:27:04,050
says that if you take
a function f from a

330
00:27:04,050 --> 00:27:08,370
to b on some interval
like this and the function

331
00:27:08,370 --> 00:27:33,920
is differentiable, then there
exists an x, where x is strictly

332
00:27:33,920 --> 00:27:42,840
between a and b and so
that the derivative at x

333
00:27:42,840 --> 00:27:46,120
is equal to the
difference quotient, where

334
00:27:46,120 --> 00:27:51,960
you do the difference
quotient at the two endpoints.

335
00:27:51,960 --> 00:27:56,040
So this here is this side of it.

336
00:27:56,040 --> 00:27:58,400
That's what you
often call the mean.

337
00:27:58,400 --> 00:28:01,240
And the mean is equal
to the derivative

338
00:28:01,240 --> 00:28:02,860
at some intermediate point.

339
00:28:02,860 --> 00:28:05,620
This is what they say.

340
00:28:05,620 --> 00:28:12,800
And so this seems a lot
better than Rolle's theorem.

341
00:28:12,800 --> 00:28:17,400
But it's an easy consequence
of Rolle's theorem.

342
00:28:17,400 --> 00:28:19,200
Because if you look at--

343
00:28:24,680 --> 00:28:31,008
so suppose you take the
function-- so proof.

344
00:28:34,920 --> 00:28:40,600
Suppose you take
the function h of x

345
00:28:40,600 --> 00:28:55,960
equal to f of x minus f
of b minus f of a divided

346
00:28:55,960 --> 00:28:59,620
by b minus a times x minus a.

347
00:28:59,620 --> 00:29:04,120
Suppose you're looking
at this function here, h.

348
00:29:04,120 --> 00:29:07,420
Then observe first
that-- so this one,

349
00:29:07,420 --> 00:29:09,440
this function is differentiable.

350
00:29:09,440 --> 00:29:12,360
This is just a constant.

351
00:29:12,360 --> 00:29:16,400
This is just a linear function.

352
00:29:16,400 --> 00:29:18,120
a is a constant also.

353
00:29:18,120 --> 00:29:20,530
So this is differentiable.

354
00:29:20,530 --> 00:29:21,370
This is a constant.

355
00:29:21,370 --> 00:29:22,990
This is differentiable.

356
00:29:22,990 --> 00:29:25,730
And we're subtracting it from
a differentiable function.

357
00:29:25,730 --> 00:29:30,012
So h is differentiable.

358
00:29:30,012 --> 00:29:34,990
h of a is f of a.

359
00:29:34,990 --> 00:29:43,390
And then this thing here, minus
f of b minus f of a, b minus a--

360
00:29:43,390 --> 00:29:45,550
and then this here is a minus a.

361
00:29:45,550 --> 00:29:46,690
So this is 0.

362
00:29:46,690 --> 00:29:49,590
So this is f of a.

363
00:29:49,590 --> 00:30:02,710
h of b is f of b minus and then
f of b minus f of a, b minus a.

364
00:30:02,710 --> 00:30:07,110
And here, you plug
in b, so b minus a.

365
00:30:07,110 --> 00:30:10,420
And so you see that
these here cancel out.

366
00:30:14,730 --> 00:30:16,910
So you are subtracting this.

367
00:30:16,910 --> 00:30:20,430
But this is a minus
minus, so you're adding.

368
00:30:20,430 --> 00:30:23,030
So this is f of a.

369
00:30:23,030 --> 00:30:28,510
So you see that for this
function, the value at a and b

370
00:30:28,510 --> 00:30:31,150
are the same.

371
00:30:31,150 --> 00:30:34,260
So now we apply Rolle's theorem.

372
00:30:53,310 --> 00:31:13,510
So now we have, by Rolle's
theorem applied to h,

373
00:31:13,510 --> 00:31:21,580
then we have that h is equal
to 0-- the derivative of h

374
00:31:21,580 --> 00:31:32,372
is equal to 0 for some x, where
x is strictly between a and b.

375
00:31:32,372 --> 00:31:33,580
This is what Rolle's theorem.

376
00:31:33,580 --> 00:31:36,220
This is because this
function, h, has

377
00:31:36,220 --> 00:31:40,740
the same value at a as at b.

378
00:31:40,740 --> 00:31:45,730
Now, what is the
derivative of h?

379
00:31:45,730 --> 00:31:53,940
So the derivative of h
is the derivative of f.

380
00:31:53,940 --> 00:31:56,840
And, again, this
is just a constant.

381
00:31:56,840 --> 00:32:06,320
So we have to keep that minus
f of b minus f of a, b minus a.

382
00:32:09,840 --> 00:32:11,000
So this is just a constant.

383
00:32:11,000 --> 00:32:13,460
And then derivative
of this is just 1.

384
00:32:13,460 --> 00:32:16,330
So this is what
the derivative is.

385
00:32:16,330 --> 00:32:18,970
And so you see that--
so we have this x,

386
00:32:18,970 --> 00:32:20,970
where this derivative is 0.

387
00:32:20,970 --> 00:32:24,410
So this here is 0.

388
00:32:24,410 --> 00:32:27,650
But if this here is 0, it
means that this thing here

389
00:32:27,650 --> 00:32:29,690
is equal to that.

390
00:32:29,690 --> 00:32:32,410
And so that was the
mean value theorem.

391
00:32:32,410 --> 00:32:35,970
So this proved the
mean value theorem.

392
00:32:35,970 --> 00:32:37,770
OK.

393
00:32:37,770 --> 00:32:40,330
There's a second
mean value theorem.

394
00:32:40,330 --> 00:32:42,696
And the second one is the
Cauchy mean value theorem.

395
00:32:45,370 --> 00:32:51,010
And so this is a
little more fancy.

396
00:32:51,010 --> 00:32:52,760
So the Cauchy mean
value theorem--

397
00:33:07,330 --> 00:33:13,290
so this is the following, that
you have two functions, f and g.

398
00:33:13,290 --> 00:33:22,450
So we have f and g on some
interval from a to b into R.

399
00:33:22,450 --> 00:33:24,230
And they are both
differentiable.

400
00:33:36,090 --> 00:33:38,530
They're both differentiable.

401
00:33:38,530 --> 00:33:51,010
And then the claim is
that there exists a x--

402
00:33:51,010 --> 00:33:53,090
let's call it x0.

403
00:33:53,090 --> 00:33:56,330
Maybe I should have called it
x0 up there, it's just one.

404
00:33:56,330 --> 00:34:03,270
There exists x0 with x0
strictly between a and b.

405
00:34:08,358 --> 00:34:09,310
OK.

406
00:34:09,310 --> 00:34:16,040
And so it's something about that
the derivative of both functions

407
00:34:16,040 --> 00:34:19,679
kind of behave like the mean
value theorem at the same time.

408
00:34:19,679 --> 00:34:24,639
So it is that the derivative--

409
00:34:24,639 --> 00:34:27,600
I will explain
this in a second--

410
00:34:27,600 --> 00:34:37,440
that the derivative at this
point times g of b minus g of a

411
00:34:37,440 --> 00:34:52,239
is equal to the derivative of
g at x0 f of b minus f of a.

412
00:34:52,239 --> 00:34:58,980
So this is the Cauchy
mean value theorem.

413
00:34:58,980 --> 00:35:01,840
So let's say that for this here.

414
00:35:01,840 --> 00:35:05,560
And let's just look
at that expression

415
00:35:05,560 --> 00:35:09,220
here before we prove the
Cauchy mean value theorem.

416
00:35:17,400 --> 00:35:25,480
So as a consequence--
so observe that,

417
00:35:25,480 --> 00:35:48,070
if g of b is not equal to g of
a and g prime at x0 is not equal

418
00:35:48,070 --> 00:35:48,570
to 0--

419
00:35:52,440 --> 00:35:55,860
if you have that, then
you could divide--

420
00:35:55,860 --> 00:35:58,340
you could have this expression.

421
00:35:58,340 --> 00:36:03,040
Let's say that-- then, by
just dividing this here

422
00:36:03,040 --> 00:36:05,600
on both sides and this
here on both sides,

423
00:36:05,600 --> 00:36:12,590
you would get that f prime of
x0 divided by g prime at x0

424
00:36:12,590 --> 00:36:24,450
is equal to f of b minus f
of a g of b minus g of a.

425
00:36:27,790 --> 00:36:31,070
You would have that.

426
00:36:31,070 --> 00:36:35,630
Now, I could think
about this thing here

427
00:36:35,630 --> 00:36:46,350
as f of b minus f of a
divided by b minus a.

428
00:36:46,350 --> 00:36:48,910
So I could think
about it like this

429
00:36:48,910 --> 00:36:57,750
and then times b minus
a, g of b minus g of a.

430
00:36:57,750 --> 00:37:03,270
And so you see that this
here, it's something about--

431
00:37:03,270 --> 00:37:06,150
the actual mean value
theorem say that this thing

432
00:37:06,150 --> 00:37:09,990
here, that there would be some
point, x0, in between that

433
00:37:09,990 --> 00:37:12,190
was equal to this.

434
00:37:12,190 --> 00:37:15,590
That would be the mean
value theorem applied to f.

435
00:37:15,590 --> 00:37:18,830
And the mean value
theorem applied to g

436
00:37:18,830 --> 00:37:24,910
would be that there exists
some x0 so that 1 over this

437
00:37:24,910 --> 00:37:28,630
is equal to this thing here.

438
00:37:28,630 --> 00:37:30,310
And the Cauchy
mean value theorem

439
00:37:30,310 --> 00:37:34,030
say that you can arrange so
that both of these things

440
00:37:34,030 --> 00:37:35,190
happen at the same time.

441
00:37:38,270 --> 00:37:40,348
So this seems
actually pretty good.

442
00:37:40,348 --> 00:37:41,640
You can do it at the same time.

443
00:37:41,640 --> 00:37:43,590
It's a little bit
surprising, in a way.

444
00:37:43,590 --> 00:37:50,190
But the proof is quite trivial.

445
00:37:50,190 --> 00:37:52,470
But it's useful.

446
00:37:52,470 --> 00:37:58,890
So the proof is the following,
that you just look--

447
00:37:58,890 --> 00:38:00,830
so this is what
we want to prove.

448
00:38:00,830 --> 00:38:05,350
So you just define--

449
00:38:05,350 --> 00:38:07,580
and the exterior is unknown.

450
00:38:07,580 --> 00:38:11,800
So if I take this expression
that I want to prove,

451
00:38:11,800 --> 00:38:17,900
if I move this thing here
over on the other side,

452
00:38:17,900 --> 00:38:19,000
then I would get--

453
00:38:19,000 --> 00:38:30,780
so in this case, I would define
h of x to be f of x g b minus g

454
00:38:30,780 --> 00:38:41,780
a minus g of x f b minus f a.

455
00:38:41,780 --> 00:38:44,480
So I would define it like this.

456
00:38:44,480 --> 00:38:51,860
And you see that when you
now take the derivative of h,

457
00:38:51,860 --> 00:38:53,240
you just get this here.

458
00:39:02,780 --> 00:39:07,180
This is because everything
else is constant.

459
00:39:07,180 --> 00:39:09,780
So this is what you get.

460
00:39:09,780 --> 00:39:14,800
And you want to find a x0
where you want to find--

461
00:39:14,800 --> 00:39:25,560
so want to find x0 where
this thing here is 0.

462
00:39:25,560 --> 00:39:28,620
To find such a x0,
it's the same as saying

463
00:39:28,620 --> 00:39:31,020
that you find an x0 over here.

464
00:39:34,620 --> 00:39:35,540
So now let's see.

465
00:39:35,540 --> 00:39:39,260
What is the value of h at a?

466
00:39:39,260 --> 00:39:43,740
If you plug in a
here, then you get

467
00:39:43,740 --> 00:39:51,820
f of a, g of b
minus g of a minus

468
00:39:51,820 --> 00:39:57,316
g of a f of b minus f of a.

469
00:39:59,900 --> 00:40:10,090
And so now you see here,
I have this one here.

470
00:40:10,090 --> 00:40:14,650
I have f of a times g of a.

471
00:40:14,650 --> 00:40:16,890
That comes with a minus sign.

472
00:40:16,890 --> 00:40:21,590
I have the same term over here,
but that comes with a plus.

473
00:40:21,590 --> 00:40:23,850
So those two cancel out.

474
00:40:23,850 --> 00:40:36,130
So I just get f of a g
of b minus g of a f of b.

475
00:40:36,130 --> 00:40:39,530
So that's what I get there.

476
00:40:39,530 --> 00:40:45,290
And now I look at h of b.

477
00:40:45,290 --> 00:40:52,050
So if I plug in b
up here for x, then

478
00:40:52,050 --> 00:41:05,600
I get f of b g of b minus
g of a minus g of b f

479
00:41:05,600 --> 00:41:08,220
of b minus f of a.

480
00:41:12,010 --> 00:41:16,200
And now you see this
when you multiply

481
00:41:16,200 --> 00:41:22,080
this by this, that term, f of
g of b, you have this over here

482
00:41:22,080 --> 00:41:24,500
also, but it comes
with a minus sign.

483
00:41:24,500 --> 00:41:26,440
So those cancel out.

484
00:41:26,440 --> 00:41:28,680
And so you're getting a term--

485
00:41:28,680 --> 00:41:30,880
the only term that
lives is this one times

486
00:41:30,880 --> 00:41:35,720
that, so minus g of a f of b.

487
00:41:35,720 --> 00:41:38,320
And then you get this
term, minus minus,

488
00:41:38,320 --> 00:41:42,500
so plus g of b, f of a.

489
00:41:48,800 --> 00:41:49,340
Right.

490
00:41:49,340 --> 00:41:49,882
That's right.

491
00:41:49,882 --> 00:41:50,400
Sorry.

492
00:41:50,400 --> 00:41:54,160
And now you see that this
here is the same as that.

493
00:41:54,160 --> 00:41:58,420
And these two here are the same.

494
00:42:01,700 --> 00:42:11,340
So we from this, we conclude
that h of a is equal to h of b.

495
00:42:11,340 --> 00:42:15,560
So again, it's satisfying the
requirements of Rolle's theorem.

496
00:42:15,560 --> 00:42:17,960
And so there is this
